  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Tree Bacon (Punctularia strigosozonata) is a wood-decay fungus in the Corticiaceae family, characterized by its distinctive striped or grooved surface that resembles crispy bacon. It forms thin, crust-like fruiting bodies on dead hardwood throughout New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Striped Surface Pattern",
      "description": "The fruiting body displays prominent radial grooves and ridges creating a corrugated, bacon-like texture that is diagnostic for this species",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Thin, Adnate Fruiting Body",
      "description": "Forms a thin, flat crust that adheres closely to the wood substrate, typically beige to pale brown in color",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Microscopic Features",
      "description": "Contains distinctive basidia and spores; examination under magnification reveals characteristic fungal structures of the Corticiaceae family",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Tree Bacon grows on dead hardwood branches and logs in moist forest environments throughout New England, playing an important role in wood decomposition.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The common name 'Tree Bacon' perfectly describes its appearance—the radial striations resemble the crispy, rippled texture of cooked bacon, making it one of fungi's most appetizing-sounding names despite being inedible.",
